{"author":{"id":"40e3ba2788b3b9fc163c354afc44e5f9d225bcf0a27e679aaa878dd6135c6dcb"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1783890229,"nonce":"MsomVKo6TCHRb0hBQtwXdFhfaIs=","title":"Phase 10: --ingest v3 approach B -- one entry per unique chunk (master-block backed)","message":"Follow-up to 59bec0d (approach A shipped 2026-07-12).\n\nApproach A writes one 'part-N' central-directory entry per chunk *occurrence* in stream order, with repeats sharing a stored block (payload dedup). The drawback the issue predicted: for highly-redundant streams the central directory grows with occurrences, not unique chunks, so listings are long and the cdir itself is large.\n\nApproach B: emit one entry per *unique* chunk plus a compact ordered manifest (chunk-index sequence) so the stream is reassembled from the manifest. Options: (i) a manifest stored as a distinguished archive entry that --ingest-restore reads, or (ii) lean on the master-block model so unique chunks live in a shared block and entries reference them. This shrinks the cdir to O(unique chunks) and makes 'uc2 -l' show only distinct content.\n\nConstraints: must stay a real UC2 archive (uc2 -l / -x still work); must keep byte-exact restore; keep the v1/v2/v3-A restore auto-detection. Cross-check against the reader's LOCATION/masterPrefix semantics in decompress.c (NoMaster=1, FirstMaster\u003e=2).\n\nAlso relates to bd13adb (tar-entry preservation), which is the higher-value semantic layer on top of the chunk model.","files":null}]}